1️⃣ Comparison Operators — compare two values
Operator	Meaning	Example
=	equal	country = 'Germany'
<> / !=	not equal	country <> 'Germany'
>	greater than	score > 500
>=	greater than or equal	score >= 500
<	less than	score < 500
<=	less than or equal	score <= 500
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country = 'Germany';
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country != 'Germany';

SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score >  500;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score >= 500;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score <  500;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score <= 500;
💡 <> is the ANSI standard — works in every database. != also works nearly everywhere.

2️⃣ Logical Operators — combine conditions (AND / OR / NOT)
Operator	TRUE when...
AND	both sides are TRUE
OR	at least one side is TRUE
NOT	the condition is FALSE
-- Both conditions must be true
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country = 'USA' AND score > 500;

-- Either condition can be true
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country = 'USA' OR score > 500;

-- NOT flips the condition (same as score >= 500)
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE NOT score < 500;
⚡ Precedence (order of evaluation)
NOT  >  AND  >  OR
(highest)          (lowest)
-- AND binds tighter than OR:
--   a OR b AND c   means   a OR (b AND c)
-- Always use ( ) when mixing AND and OR!
WHERE country = 'USA' OR (country = 'Germany' AND score > 500)
3️⃣ BETWEEN — range check (boundaries are INCLUSIVE)
BETWEEN low AND high   -- includes both low AND high
-- Both of these are exactly the same:
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score BETWEEN 100 AND 500;
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE score >= 100 AND score <= 500;
⚠️ Boundaries are INCLUSIVE — 100 and 500 themselves are included. 💡 Works with dates too: BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-12-31'

4️⃣ IN / NOT IN — membership in a list
IN is just shorthand for multiple ORs.

-- These two are identical:
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country = 'Germany' OR country = 'USA';
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country IN ('Germany', 'USA');

-- Exclude a list
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country NOT IN ('Germany', 'USA');
💡 Cleaner and shorter than chained ORs — easy to read and easy to add values. ⚠️ NOT IN with a NULL in the list returns nothing (traps beginners) — filter NULLs out first.

5️⃣ LIKE — search with wildcards (pattern matching)
Wildcard	Meaning
%	any characters (zero, one, or many)
_	exactly one character
-- Starts with M
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE 'M%';

-- Ends with n
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE '%n';

-- 3rd character is 'r', anything before/after
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE '__r%';

-- Exactly 4 letters, starting with M:  M_ _ _
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE 'M___';

-- Contains 'ria' anywhere
SELECT * FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE '%ria%';
📊 Pattern cheat table
Pattern	Matches	Does NOT match
M%	Maria, Mohan, M	Amara
%n	Nayan, John, n	Nadia
M_	Mo, Me	Moa (too long)
_ _r%	Maria (aria), Sara	Ram (r is 1st char)
%ria%	Maria, Gloria	Rita
⚠️ LIKE is usually case-sensitive in PostgreSQL, case-insensitive in SQL Server/MySQL by default. ⚠️ LIKE '%...%' can't use indexes → slow on big tables.

